+/// Slices a string to at most `max` Unicode **codepoints**, never
+/// splitting a multi-byte character.
+///
+/// This is deliberately NOT `string.slice` (which counts graphemes):
+/// the DB CHECK constraints bound `length()` — Postgres character
+/// count — so a grapheme-bounded slice of a multi-codepoint header
+/// (ZWJ emoji, combining marks, flags) could exceed the bound and 500
+/// the write path. Codepoint slicing keeps the stored value within the
+/// CHECK bound for every input.
+///
+/// Two deliberate consequences:
+///
+/// - The cut may land mid-grapheme (e.g. 512 codepoints of a ZWJ
+///   family emoji leaves a dangling first member). Harmless — these
+///   values are stored for evidentiary/fraud comparisons, never
+///   displayed or parsed.
+/// - Headers are raw bytes; on a byte that is not a valid UTF-8
+///   codepoint the decoder stops and the remainder is dropped
+///   (truncation, not a crash). The value is then always valid UTF-8
+///   within the bound.
+fn slice_codepoint_bounded(value: String, max: Int) -> String {
+  value
+  |> string.to_utf_codepoints
+  |> list.take(max)
+  |> string.from_utf_codepoints
+}
